Q={}
def q(c,p,correct,wrong1,why1,wrong2,why2):
 Q.setdefault('python.'+c,[]).append((p,correct,[(wrong1,why1),(wrong2,why2)]))
q('variables_assignment','After cost = quantity * unit_price, quantity is reassigned. Which action updates cost to the product of the current named values?','Assign cost = quantity * unit_price again.','Do nothing: cost automatically tracks quantity.','Assignment stores the expression’s value at that assignment, rather than a continuing formula.','Assign quantity = cost.','That rebinds quantity to the old cost rather than recalculating cost.')
q('core_data_types','Which statement correctly distinguishes 3 + 4.5 from "3" + "4.5"?','The first is numeric addition with a float result; the second concatenates text.','Both concatenate text.','Unquoted numeric values use numeric arithmetic, not string concatenation.','Both add numbers and produce a float.','Quoted values are strings, and + concatenates those strings.')
q('core_data_types','What kind of value does the numeric comparison quantity >= 3 produce?','A bool, either True or False.','A string containing the expression.','A numeric comparison produces a boolean, not text.','The integer quantity itself.','Comparison tests the values; it does not return the quantity as its result.')
q('arithmetic_operators','Which expression assigns the cost of quantity items at unit_price each?','cost = quantity * unit_price','cost = quantity + unit_price','Addition combines the two amounts once; multiplication applies the unit price to the quantity.','cost = quantity / unit_price','Division is not multiplication of quantity by the per-item price.')
q('comparison_operators','Which group correctly expresses equal, unequal, strictly above and at least the threshold 3?','quantity == 3; quantity != 3; quantity > 3; quantity >= 3','quantity = 3; quantity != 3; quantity >= 3; quantity > 3','Single = assigns rather than compares, and the strict and inclusive checks are swapped.','quantity == 3; quantity == 3; quantity < 3; quantity <= 3','Equality is not inequality, and below checks do not express above thresholds.')
q('comparison_operators','When quantity equals its threshold, which comparison excludes that equality?','quantity > 3','quantity >= 3','>= includes equality.','quantity == 3','== explicitly tests equality rather than excluding it.')
q('logical_operators','For False and quantity_ok, which statement describes the result and evaluation of the right operand?','The result is False and quantity_ok is not evaluated.','The result is True and quantity_ok is evaluated.','and with a false left operand short-circuits with that false value.','The result is False but quantity_ok must still be evaluated.','and skips its right operand when its left operand is false.')
q('logical_operators','To allow a request only if budget_ok is true and either quantity_ok or label_ok is true, which grouping fits?','budget_ok and (quantity_ok or label_ok)','(budget_ok and quantity_ok) or label_ok','This permits label_ok alone to bypass the budget requirement.','budget_ok or (quantity_ok and label_ok)','This allows budget_ok alone and does not enforce the stated combined requirement.')
q('type_conversion','Which rule describes int(-3.8)?','It truncates toward zero.','It always rounds downward.','Integer conversion truncates toward zero rather than flooring a negative value.','It rounds to the nearest integer.','int conversion discards the fractional part rather than applying nearest rounding.')
q('type_conversion','After quantity = float(quantity_text), what happens to quantity_text?','Its original text binding remains unchanged.','It now holds the converted float too.','The returned conversion is assigned to quantity, not quantity_text.','It is removed because its value was converted.','Conversion returns a new result and does not remove the original binding.')
q('conditional_statements','A grade chain awards A at scores >= 90, B at scores >= 70 but < 90, C otherwise. Which order works when using >= threshold conditions?','Test >= 90 first, then >= 70, then else.','Test >= 70 first, then >= 90, then else.','The earlier >= 70 branch would also catch scores deserving A.','Use two independent if statements followed by else.','Independent if statements are not the required exclusive if/elif/else chain.')
q('while_loops','A loop tests response != "done". What must its body do to let a later entered done terminate it?','Reassign response from a fresh input request.','Keep response unchanged and only test it again.','An unchanged non-done response keeps the same condition true.','Assign the fresh input only to a different name.','The loop condition reads response, so updating an unrelated name does not update that condition.')
q('lists','For an ordered mutable labels list, which expression selects the second and third items but excludes the fourth?','labels[1:3]','labels[1:4]','Slice stop is exclusive; stop 4 also includes the fourth item at index 3.','labels[2:3]','Indexing starts at zero, so this includes only the third item.')
q('lists','Which pair of operations replaces the first label with blue, then adds yellow at the end?','labels[0] = "blue"; labels.append("yellow")','labels.append("blue"); labels[0] = "yellow"','append adds at the end; this does not replace the first item with blue.','labels[1] = "blue"; labels.append("yellow")','Index 1 identifies the second item, not the first.')
q('sets','What happens to repeated red entries when first = set(["red", "blue", "red"])?','The set retains unique labels only.','The set keeps both red entries in their original positions.','Sets store unique elements and do not provide positional sequence traversal.','The repeated red label is entirely removed.','Deduplication keeps one occurrence as an element, rather than removing the label altogether.')
q('dictionaries','A quantities dictionary has notebook and pen keys. Which lookup retrieves the notebook value, and what happens for an absent key?','quantities["notebook"] retrieves it; an absent key raises KeyError.','quantities[0] retrieves the first product and absent keys return zero.','Dictionary lookup uses keys, not sequence positions, and missing keys do not default to zero.','quantities["notebook"] retrieves it; absent keys are added automatically.','A missing-key lookup raises KeyError rather than inserting an entry.')
q('dictionaries','For an existing notebook key, what does quantities["notebook"] = 5 do?','It replaces the value associated with notebook.','It adds a second notebook key beside the first.','Dictionary keys are unique; assigning an existing key replaces its associated value.','It changes every product value to 5.','Subscription assignment targets the named key, not every key.')
q('string_basics_formatting','Which literal inserts quantity into the surrounding sentence?','f"Quantity: {quantity}"','"Quantity: {quantity}"','Without the f prefix, braces and the name remain literal text.','f"Quantity: quantity"','Without braces, quantity is literal text rather than an inserted expression.')
q('defining_calling_functions','After defining greet, which statement invokes its body?','greet()','greet','Naming the function without call parentheses does not invoke it.','def greet():','def introduces the definition, rather than calling an already defined function.')
q('parameters_arguments','In def describe(label, quantity): followed by describe("pen", 2), which are the parameters?','label and quantity; the arguments are pen and 2.','pen and 2; the arguments are label and quantity.','Parameters are the names in the definition, not the values at the call.','Only quantity is a parameter; pen binds to it first.','Both definition names are parameters, and positional arguments bind in the listed order.')
q('return_values','A helper displays a product with print but reaches its end without return. What does assigning its call to cost store?','None, not the displayed product.','The displayed product.','Displaying a value is not supplying the function’s calculated return value.','The helper’s parameter values as a pair.','No pair is returned automatically; reaching the end without return yields None.')
q('tuples','Which expression creates an ordered, immutable one-item tuple containing label?','(label,)','(label)','Parentheses alone group an expression; the trailing comma makes a one-item tuple.','[label]','Brackets create a mutable list rather than a tuple.')
q('tuples','A pair contains two values. Which assignment unpacks both in order?','smallest, largest = pair','smallest, middle, largest = pair','Three targets do not match the pair’s two values.','smallest = pair','This binds the whole pair to one name rather than unpacking its two items.')
q('function_default_keyword_args','For def cost(quantity, price, fee=2), which call supplies required arguments without binding a parameter twice?','cost(3, price=4)','cost(3, 4, quantity=3)','The first positional argument already binds quantity, so the keyword binds it twice.','cost(quantity=3)','price is required and has not been supplied.')
q('variable_scope','Inside a function with a local quantity, how can it explicitly read the same-named module binding?','globals()["quantity"]','quantity','Ordinary quantity resolves to the local binding when one exists.','Assign quantity first and assume that changed the module binding.','Ordinary local assignment changes the local binding, not the module entry.')
q('variable_scope','Which comparison of assignment effects is correct?','Ordinary local assignment changes the local binding; global quantity makes assignment target the module binding.','Both kinds always change the module binding.','An ordinary function-assigned name is local unless declared otherwise.','global quantity creates an independent local binding.','global directs the name to the module binding rather than making a new local binding.')
q('error_handling','Which layout protects quantity = int(text) with ValueError recovery?','Put conversion in an indented try body and recovery in an aligned except ValueError body.','Put conversion before try and recovery inside try.','A conversion outside the protected body is not protected by that try.','Indent except ValueError underneath the conversion inside try.','The except header must align with try; its recovery body is indented below its own header.')
q('error_handling','What happens if the try body succeeds, versus raising an exception that does not match except ValueError?','Success skips the handler; an unmatched exception propagates.','Success runs the handler; unmatched exceptions are ignored.','The handler runs only for a matching exception, and an unmatched exception is not handled there.','The handler catches every exception because it names ValueError.','A typed handler catches matching exceptions, not arbitrary unmatched types.')
q('import_statements','After from math import cos, how is the imported function called directly for value?','cos(value)','math.cos(value), assuming this import also binds math','Importing the member binds cos directly; it does not bind the module name math.','import cos(value)','import binds names; it is not a function-call expression.')
q('pep8_style','Which PEP 8 pair gives the recommended code-line and flowing comment/docstring limits?','79 characters for code; 72 for flowing comments or docstrings.','72 for code; 79 for flowing comments or docstrings.','This reverses the two recommendations.','79 for both.','Flowing comments and docstrings have the shorter recommended limit.')
q('for_loops','In a loop that adds each quantity to total, why is total = 0 placed before the loop rather than inside it?','It initializes the running total once so each addition retains earlier items.','It must reset total each iteration to accumulate all items.','Resetting inside the loop discards the earlier accumulated items.','It makes the loop process only the final item.','Initialization before the loop does not skip iterations; for visits successive items.')
q('loop_control','A quantity loop must stop at -1 and skip zero before adding to total. Which arrangement fits?','if quantity == -1: break; if quantity == 0: continue; then add.','if quantity == -1: continue; if quantity == 0: break; then add.','The stop and skip actions are reversed.','Add first, then test break and continue.','Placing the addition first does not let the zero bypass it or the stop value end processing before addition.')
q('loop_control','Which statement correctly contrasts break and continue?','break exits the innermost loop; continue skips the rest of the current iteration.','Both end the entire loop.','continue advances to the next iteration instead of exiting the loop.','break skips only the current iteration; continue exits the loop.','This reverses the distinct effects.')
q('iterating_collections','Which traversal expectation is justified for lists, tuples and sets?','Lists and tuples follow sequence order; sets have no positional sequence order.','All three guarantee the same positional traversal.','Sets are unordered rather than positional sequences.','Lists and tuples are unordered, but sets are positional.','This reverses the sequence/set distinction.')
q('iterating_collections','What does for label in quantities visit when quantities is a dictionary?','Its keys.','Its values.','Direct dictionary iteration visits keys; it does not directly yield values.','Its key-value pairs.','Use items() to visit pairs together; direct iteration yields keys.')
q('comprehensions','Which list comprehension squares only even quantities?','[quantity * quantity for quantity in quantities if quantity % 2 == 0]','[quantity * quantity for quantity in quantities]','Without the condition it also includes odd quantities.','[quantity for quantity in quantities if quantity % 2 == 0]','This filters even values but does not compute their squares.')
q('comprehensions','Which form builds a dictionary mapping each label to a computed value?','{label: expression for label in labels}','[expression for label in labels]','Square brackets build a list, not key-value associations.','{label: expression}','This is a dictionary literal with one association, not iteration over all labels.')
q('file_io','Which open mode reads existing text without replacing its contents?','r','w','Write mode replaces existing contents rather than reading them.','Both r and w','Only r is the stated read mode; w writes and replaces contents.')

def normalized(v):
 if isinstance(v,(set,frozenset)):return sorted(v)
 if isinstance(v,tuple):return [normalized(x) for x in v]
 if isinstance(v,list):return [normalized(x) for x in v]
 if isinstance(v,dict):return {str(k):normalized(x) for k,x in v.items()}
 return v
